# CCAR-F Practice Questions — Scenarios 1–6, Refined to Hardest Difficulty

Same six scenarios, same 60 questions, same tested concepts — only the options change.
Rules applied throughout: all options are close, plausible matches to one another; the
correct answer is concise and length-matched to the distractors; no throwaway options;
correct-answer letters are rotated across the set; answer key with rationale sits
separately at the bottom.

---

## Scenario 1: Customer Support Resolution Agent

**1.1 (Domain 1)** — Which control flow correctly determines when to keep executing
tools and when to present the final response?
A) Loop until a response contains no further tool-shaped keywords
B) Run a fixed number of iterations sized to the ticket's estimated complexity
C) Inspect `stop_reason`: `"tool_use"` → execute and loop; `"end_turn"` → present the
   final response
D) Stop as soon as any response contains any text content alongside a tool call

**1.2 (Domain 1)** — How does the model actually receive tool result data?
A) Your code appends a tool-result block, referencing the tool call's ID, to the
   conversation history and resends it to Claude
B) The MCP server pushes the result directly into the model's context automatically
C) The model re-reads the tool's description, which now includes the fresh result
D) The harness re-invokes the same tool call so the model can observe it firsthand

**1.3 (Domain 1)** — A refund cap of $500 is occasionally bypassed despite prompt
instructions. Correct enforcement design?
A) Move the limit to the very top of the system prompt and repeat it every turn
B) Add the limit as a note inside the `process_refund` tool's own description
C) Lower the temperature so the model follows the stated limit more consistently
D) A hook intercepting outgoing `process_refund` calls, blocking amounts over $500 and
   redirecting them to escalation

**1.4 (Domain 1)** — A customer's message raises three concerns at once (refund,
address change, loyalty credit). How should the agent handle it?
A) Resolve only the first concern and wait for the others to be separately re-raised
B) Decompose into three items, investigate each — in parallel where possible — using
   shared context, then synthesize one unified resolution
C) Escalate the whole message immediately, since a multi-issue request exceeds a
   single-turn agent's intended scope
D) Ask the customer to resend each of the three concerns as separate messages

**1.5 (Domain 1)** — An escalation hands off to a human with no transcript access.
What should the handoff include?
A) A structured summary: customer ID, root-cause analysis, amounts/order numbers, and
   a recommended action
B) The full raw JSON of every tool result generated during the session
C) The customer's name plus a computed sentiment score for the conversation
D) A one-line note that the case was escalated, so the human starts fresh

**1.6 (Domain 2)** — `process_refund` failure returns the generic text "Operation
failed," causing wrong retry behavior. Fix?
A) Instruct the agent, via the tool description, to retry failed calls exactly twice
B) Return structured error metadata — an `errorCategory` (transient/validation/
   business/permission) plus an `isRetryable` boolean and a human-readable message
C) Have the agent apologize to the customer whenever any error text appears
D) Remove failure responses from the tool entirely so retries stop triggering

**1.7 (Domain 2)** — A refund attempt violates the returns-window policy and the tool
rejects it. Which error response is correct?
A) `errorCategory: "transient"`, so the agent automatically retries the same call later
B) An empty result marked as success, so the agent moves on without further action
C) `errorCategory: "business"`, `retriable: false`, with a customer-friendly explanation
D) The tool returns a raw stack trace so downstream logging can capture full detail

**1.8 (Domain 2, select TWO)** — The agent confuses `get_customer` and
`lookup_order`. Which TWO description changes fix the misrouting?
A) Shorten both descriptions so the model reads them fully before deciding
B) Document each tool's expected input format and add example queries
C) Prefix both descriptions with the word "IMPORTANT:" for emphasis
D) Add explicit boundary language differentiating each tool's intended use case
E) Remove both descriptions and rely on the tool names alone to disambiguate

**1.9 (Domain 5)** — The customer's first message says: "I want to speak to a human.
Do not try to fix this yourself." The issue itself looks routine. What should the agent do?
A) Run sentiment analysis first and escalate only if frustration exceeds a threshold
B) Ask three clarifying questions before deciding whether escalation is warranted
C) Attempt resolution first, since the issue looks routine, and escalate only if that
   attempt fails
D) Escalate immediately — an explicit customer request for a human is honored without
   first attempting investigation

**1.10 (Domain 5, select TWO)** — Long sessions misquote amounts and order numbers
because lossy summarization drops detail, and tool results dump 40+ fields into context
at once. Which TWO changes fix this?
A) Extract the transactional facts into a persistent case-facts block kept outside the
   summarized history
B) Increase `max_tokens` so more of each tool result survives into the response
C) Summarize more aggressively at each step to keep the conversation compact
D) Trim each tool result to only the fields relevant to the current issue before it
   ever enters context
E) Ask the customer to repeat the key numbers back at the end of the call

