## Scenario 2: Code Generation with Claude Code

**2.1 (Domain 3)** — A personal `/scratch-notes` command shouldn't appear for
teammates. Where does it belong?
A) `.claude/commands/` inside the repo, so it travels with every clone
B) Root `CLAUDE.md`, documented under a `# Commands` heading
C) `~/.claude/commands/` in the home directory — user-scoped, outside version control
D) `.claude/rules/`, gated behind a username-matched path glob

**2.2 (Domain 3)** — `/analyze-architecture` produces thousands of lines that pollute
the main conversation. Which frontmatter option fixes this?
A) `allowed-tools`, narrowed to only the tools the analysis actually needs
B) `argument-hint`, so the skill is invoked with a narrower, pre-scoped target
C) `context: fork` — runs the skill in an isolated sub-agent context and returns only
   the results
D) `paths`, scoping the skill to load only for architecture-relevant directories

**2.3 (Domain 3)** — Guarantee a skill can create files but can never run shell
commands. How?
A) State "never run Bash" directly inside the skill's own instructions
B) Run the skill only while the session is in plan mode
C) Set `allowed-tools` in frontmatter to permit only the needed file-write operations,
   excluding Bash
D) Trust the skill's narrow stated purpose to keep it from reaching for Bash

**2.4 (Domain 3)** — Team standards live in your personal `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`;
teammates' sessions ignore them. Fix and verification?
A) Paste the standards at the start of every session as a standing habit
B) Move the standards into the project-level `CLAUDE.md`, checked into the repo; use
   `/memory` to verify which memory files a given session actually loaded
C) Have each teammate copy your personal `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` file onto their own machine
D) Email the contents of your personal file to the team as reference documentation

**2.5 (Domain 3)** — Project `CLAUDE.md` has grown to 2,000 lines and loads in full
regardless of the task at hand. Recommended reorganization?
A) Move the entire file's contents up to a user-level `CLAUDE.md` instead
B) Convert the whole file into a single manual slash command invoked on demand
C) Split it into focused topic files under `.claude/rules/` (testing.md,
   api-conventions.md, deployment.md) with path-scoped frontmatter
D) Delete the sections that seem least frequently relevant to recent tasks

**2.6 (Domain 1)** — Continue exactly where yesterday's named session `leak-hunt` left
off. What do you run?
A) A new session, with yesterday's scrollback pasted in as the opening message
B) `claude --resume leak-hunt`
C) `claude -p "continue the leak investigation"`, letting the harness infer the session
D) `claude --continue-all`, to restore every recent session's state at once

**2.7 (Domain 1)** — Compare two competing refactoring strategies independently from
the same analysis baseline, with no cross-contamination between them. Which capability?
A) `/compact` run between exploring the first strategy and the second
B) The same session, asking Claude to "forget" the first strategy before the second
C) Two brand-new sessions, each redoing the baseline analysis from scratch
D) `fork_session` — independent branches from the shared baseline

**2.8 (Domain 1)** — Resuming last week's session, but merged changes mean most tool
results now describe code that no longer exists. Reliable approach?
A) Resume as-is and increase `max_tokens`, since more budget compensates for drift
B) Never resume any session once the underlying code has changed at all
C) A new session, seeded with a structured summary of prior conclusions — stale tool
   results make raw resumption unreliable here
D) Resume as-is; Claude auto-detects which files changed since the session paused

**2.9 (Domain 5)** — Context is filling with verbose output mid-session, and the
session isn't ready to end. Which built-in command helps?
A) `/rules`, to reload only the currently-relevant configuration files
B) `/fork`, to branch off a fresh context from this point forward
C) `/compact` — compacts the conversation so the session can continue productively
D) `/memory`, to inspect which memory files are contributing to context size

**2.10 (Domain 5, select TWO)** — The agent starts answering from "typical patterns"
instead of specifics discovered earlier in long sessions. Which TWO practices
counteract this?
A) Switch to a larger context window and keep accumulating without other changes
B) Maintain a scratchpad file recording key findings; have the agent reference it
   for later questions
C) Ask the agent to be more specific whenever a generic-sounding answer appears
D) Repeat important questions a second time to reinforce them
E) Delegate verbose investigation to subagents that return summaries, keeping the main
   session focused on coordination

