## Scenario 3: Multi-Agent Research System

**3.1 (Domain 1)** — The coordinator's system prompt says to delegate, but it never
spawns subagents and attempts all research itself. Cause?
A) The model tier in use is too small to reliably decide when to delegate
B) Subagents in this framework must always be started manually by a human operator
C) Coordinator's `allowedTools` doesn't include `Task` — the mechanism for spawning
   subagents
D) The prompt needs the word "delegate" written in capital letters for emphasis

**3.2 (Domain 1)** — A web-search subagent gathers strong findings; the synthesis
subagent's reports are generic and reference nothing specific. The coordinator's
delegation prompt just says "Synthesize the research findings." What's wrong?
A) The synthesis agent needs its own web-search tools to verify claims independently
B) Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's conversation history — the coordinator
   must include the actual findings directly in the synthesis delegation prompt
C) The synthesis agent needs extended thinking enabled to reason more thoroughly
D) The delegation prompt needs softer, more polite phrasing to engage the model fully

**3.3 (Domain 1)** — The coordinator invokes a search subagent, waits, then invokes an
analysis subagent — doubling latency despite the two being independent. How to
parallelize?
A) Run two entirely separate copies of the whole multi-agent system side by side
B) Ask each subagent, via its prompt, to complete its work faster
C) Emit both `Task` calls within a single response, spawning both subagents in parallel
D) Accept that subagent parallelism isn't achievable within one coordinator turn

**3.4 (Domain 1)** — The coordinator gives subagents rigid, step-by-step scripts, and
they fail whenever a topic doesn't fit the script. Better design?
A) Have subagents ask a human operator whenever the script's next step doesn't apply
B) Specify research goals and quality criteria instead, letting each subagent adapt
   its own approach to the topic
C) Make the step-by-step procedures longer, covering more edge cases explicitly
D) Remove all instructions from the subagent prompts entirely

**3.5 (Domain 1)** — Reports come back coherent but shallow on certain subtopics, and
the team wants self-detection and repair of coverage gaps. Which pattern?
A) Always run every subagent exactly twice and merge both sets of results
B) An iterative refinement loop: the coordinator evaluates the synthesis for gaps,
   re-delegates targeted follow-up queries, and re-invokes synthesis until sufficient
C) Accept the first synthesis pass as final, since re-running risks introducing drift
D) Ask the report generator to pad thin sections with general background knowledge

**3.6 (Domain 2)** — Every subagent gets the full 18-tool catalog; the synthesis agent
tries calling web search, and the search agent tries document parsing. Correct
redesign?
A) Add a 19th coordinating tool whose job is telling other agents which tool to use
B) Write longer prompts for each subagent, explicitly warning it off certain tools
C) Alphabetize the shared tool catalog so relevant tools appear earlier in the list
D) Scope each subagent's tool set to its actual role — a handful of relevant tools each

**3.7 (Domain 2)** — Subagents burn many calls just discovering what data exists
before real work begins. Which MCP capability reduces this?
A) Hard-code the current data inventory directly into every subagent's system prompt
B) Expose content catalogs — document hierarchies, schemas — as MCP resources, giving
   visibility without exploratory tool calls
C) Remove the subagents' ability to explore the data source at all
D) Increase each subagent's allotted tool-call budget so discovery has more room

**3.8 (Domain 5)** — Final reports cite nothing, because each summarization step
strips source attribution along the way. Structural fix?
A) Reduce the number of summarization steps in the pipeline down to exactly one
B) Instruct the synthesis step to append "sources available on request" at the end
C) Require subagents to output structured claim-source mappings — claim, evidence,
   source — that every downstream agent preserves through synthesis
D) Have the report generator append plausible-looking citations after the fact

**3.9 (Domain 5, select TWO)** — Two credible sources report different market-size
figures, and one subagent simply picked the larger number. Which TWO practices are
correct?
A) Annotate the conflict explicitly — both values and their attribution — and let the
   coordinator decide on reconciliation
B) Average the two figures together and report the mean as the answer
C) Require publication or data-collection dates in structured outputs, so temporal
   differences aren't misread as contradictions
D) Always adopt whichever source is more recent and silently discard the other
E) Exclude both conflicting sources from the final report entirely

**3.10 (Domain 5, select TWO)** — How should the web-search subagent behave when
searches fail or return nothing? Which TWO behaviors are correct?
A) Attempt local recovery for transient failures, and propagate only unresolved
   errors, including what was attempted and any partial results
B) Report every problem uniformly as a generic "search unavailable" message
C) Terminate the entire multi-agent workflow the moment any single search fails
D) Distinguish access failures (timeouts, errors) from genuinely valid empty results
   (a successful query that simply found no matches)
E) Return empty results marked as a successful search whenever a failure occurs

