## Scenario 5: Claude Code for Continuous Integration

**5.1 (Domain 3)** — A CI job must run Claude Code non-interactively and produce
review findings the pipeline can parse and post as inline PR comments. Which
invocation is correct?
A) `claude "review this PR"`, piped through `grep` to extract findings from the prose
B) `claude --batch "review this PR" --format=structured`
C) `claude -p "review this PR"` with `--output-format json` and `--json-schema`,
   producing machine-parseable structured findings without waiting on interactive input
D) `claude -p "review this PR"` alone, followed by regex-parsing of the prose output

**5.2 (Domain 3)** — CI-generated tests ignore the team's fixture library, duplicate
helper setup, and test trivialities; developers reject most of them. What is the
configuration-level fix?
A) Lower the temperature of the generation call so output is more conservative
B) Generate three times as many tests, on the assumption some will survive review
C) Have developers rewrite the generated tests as a standing recurring chore
D) Document testing standards, what makes a test valuable, and the available fixtures
   in `CLAUDE.md` — the mechanism that supplies project context to CI-invoked runs

**5.3 (Domain 3)** — Reviews re-run after each new commit to a PR, and the bot
re-posts the same findings every time; developers have started muting it. How should
re-reviews be designed?
A) Limit reviews to exactly one per PR, regardless of any later commits
B) Post all the same findings again, reformatted slightly so they look new
C) Review only the newest commit's diff, completely in isolation from prior reviews
D) Include the prior review's findings in context and instruct the model to report
   only new issues, or previously reported ones that remain unaddressed

**5.4 (Domain 3)** — The same Claude Code session that generated a change also
reviews it, and the review is conspicuously gentle, missing issues an independent
reviewer catches. Why, and what is the design implication?
A) Generation and review should simply run on different calendar days
B) A session retains the reasoning context from generation, making it less likely to
   question its own decisions — CI reviews should run in an independent instance
   without the generator's context
C) Reviews should always be performed on a strictly larger model tier than generation
D) The model is merely being polite; instruct it directly to be harsher

**5.5 (Domain 4)** — The review prompt says "be conservative and only report
high-confidence findings," yet false positives remain high. What does effective
precision engineering look like instead?
A) Reduce the number of files reviewed per run to lower total findings
B) Add "be very, very conservative" to the prompt for stronger emphasis
C) Report all findings, but sort them by the model's own stated confidence
D) Replace confidence language with explicit categorical criteria — which issue types
   to report, which to skip — with concrete boundaries

**5.6 (Domain 4)** — Findings in the "code style" category are 70% false positives,
and developers have begun dismissing security findings too — trust in the whole
reviewer is collapsing. What is the right operational move?
A) Rename "code style" to "code quality" to reset developer perception
B) Temporarily disable the style category to restore trust in the accurate
   categories, while improving the style prompts before re-enabling it
C) Ship more style findings so the category demonstrates its own importance over time
D) Ask developers to be more tolerant of noise while the category is tuned

**5.7 (Domain 4)** — The reviewer labels near-identical issues "critical" one day and
"minor" the next, making severity-based merge gates unreliable. How do you get
consistent severity classification?
A) Map severity directly to the line count of the affected code change
B) Remove severity levels entirely and treat every finding equally
C) Let the model choose severity freely, but average its choice over five runs
D) Define explicit severity criteria with concrete code examples for each level, so
   every severity has an operational definition the model can match against

**5.8 (Domain 4)** — The reviewer keeps flagging accepted idiomatic patterns (e.g.,
intentional fall-throughs with comments) as bugs, and detailed prose instructions
haven't fixed it. What is the most effective addition?
A) A blanket rule that anything containing a code comment is acceptable
B) A ban on reviewing any file that contains an idiomatic pattern at all
C) Few-shot examples distinguishing the accepted patterns from genuine issues,
   showing the reasoning for why each is or isn't reportable, so the model
   generalizes the judgment to novel cases
D) An instruction to "use good judgment about idioms" in the system prompt

**5.9 (Domain 4, select TWO)** — Deciding which CI workloads to move to the Message
Batches API for its cost savings. Which TWO statements are accurate?
A) Blocking workflows such as pre-merge checks should stay on the synchronous API,
   since batches can take up to 24 hours with no latency SLA
B) The batch API supports multi-turn tool calling within a single submitted request
C) Batch processing suits latency-tolerant, non-blocking workloads like nightly test
   generation and weekly audit reports
D) Batches are guaranteed to complete within one hour of submission
E) Batch responses cannot be matched back to the requests that produced them

**5.10 (Domain 4, select TWO)** — Adding few-shot examples to the review prompt.
Which TWO practices reflect how few-shot prompting works best?
A) Include examples demonstrating the exact desired output format — location, issue,
   severity, suggested fix — to achieve consistent, actionable findings
B) Include as many examples as possible, twenty or more, to cover every case
C) Rely on examples only for prose tasks, since they don't transfer to code review
D) Use 2–4 targeted examples aimed at the genuinely ambiguous scenarios, showing the
   reasoning for why one action was chosen over plausible alternatives
E) Treat explicit criteria as unnecessary once a handful of examples has been added

