## Scenario 6: Structured Data Extraction

**6.1 (Domain 4)** — A pipeline asks Claude to "respond with JSON" in plain text;
downstream parsing breaks on markdown fences, trailing commentary, and occasional
malformed syntax. What is the most reliable structural fix?
A) Post-process the text with regexes that strip fences and repair syntax
B) Switch to XML output instead, which models are said to format more carefully
C) Strengthen the prompt to: "respond ONLY with valid JSON, no exceptions"
D) Define the extraction as a tool whose input schema is your JSON schema, and read
   the structured data from the `tool_use` block — guaranteeing schema-compliant
   output and eliminating JSON syntax errors

**6.2 (Domain 4)** — Several extraction tools exist, one per document type, and
incoming documents are of unknown type. The model must always produce a structured
extraction, choosing the appropriate schema itself, never conversational text. Which
`tool_choice` setting is correct?
A) `tool_choice` forced to one single named tool, applied to every document regardless
B) `tool_choice: "auto"` — the model decides for itself whether to call a tool at all
C) Omit `tool_choice` entirely, since the default already handles this correctly
D) `tool_choice: "any"` — the model must call some tool but may choose which,
   guaranteeing structured output while leaving schema selection to the model

**6.3 (Domain 4)** — An invoice schema marks `vendor_tax_id` as required. On invoices
that genuinely lack a tax ID, the model fabricates plausible-looking values. What is
the schema-level fix?
A) Add a prompt instruction telling the model never to fabricate tax IDs
B) Lower the temperature so any fabrication is at least less creative
C) Make the field optional/nullable, so the model can legitimately return null when
   the information is genuinely absent from the source document
D) Post-validate tax IDs against a checksum and discard any that fail it

**6.4 (Domain 4)** — Extractions are always syntactically valid, yet invoices arrive
where line items don't sum to the stated total, and values occasionally land in the
wrong fields. What should you understand and do?
A) Re-run each extraction repeatedly until the numbers happen to sum correctly
B) Make every field an unconstrained string so nothing can technically be "wrong"
C) Strict schemas eliminate syntax errors but not semantic ones — add a semantic
   validation layer, e.g., extracting a `calculated_total` alongside `stated_total`
   and flagging discrepancies with a `conflict_detected` boolean
D) Conclude schema compliance was falsely advertised and file it as a platform bug

**6.5 (Domain 4)** — An extraction fails validation with two specific field errors,
and you plan to retry. What should the retry request contain, and when should you
skip retrying altogether?
A) A different document entirely, to give the model a genuinely fresh start
B) The original document, the failed extraction, and the specific validation errors —
   enabling targeted self-correction; skip retrying when the required information is
   simply absent from the source, since no retry can produce it
C) Only the validation errors, to keep the retry request cheap, and always retry at
   least five times regardless of the cause
D) Just the instruction "try again more carefully," with no other context attached

**6.6 (Domain 4)** — An overnight batch of 10,000 documents completes with 200
failures: mostly oversized documents past context limits, plus some transient errors.
What is the correct failure-handling workflow?
A) Switch the 200 failures over to the synchronous API unchanged, on the assumption
   its context limits don't apply
B) Resubmit the entire 10,000-document batch and keep whichever set of results
   arrives back first
C) Discard the 200 failures outright as an acceptable loss rate for this volume
D) Identify the failed requests by `custom_id` and resubmit only those, with
   modifications where needed — e.g., chunking the oversized documents first

**6.7 (Domain 2)** — A single `analyze_document` tool handles extraction,
summarization, and claim verification, selected by a `mode` parameter. The agent
regularly picks the wrong mode and results are inconsistent. What is the recommended
redesign?
A) Reduce to two modes by merging extraction into summarization
B) Split the generic tool into purpose-specific tools — `extract_data_points`,
   `summarize_content`, `verify_claim_against_source` — each with a defined
   input/output contract
C) Document the three modes more thoroughly inside the single existing description
D) Add a fourth mode whose job is automatically detecting which mode to use

**6.8 (Domain 2)** — An MCP extraction tool hits a parsing failure and returns "Error:
could not parse document" as an ordinary successful text result. The agent then treats
that sentence as document content. What is the correct MCP pattern?
A) Throw an unhandled exception on failure and let the connection drop entirely
B) Return the failure with the MCP `isError` flag set, so the agent programmatically
   recognizes a tool failure instead of interpreting error prose as data
C) Return an empty string instead of an error message on failure
D) Prefix the error text with "SYSTEM ERROR:" so the agent is more likely to notice it

**6.9 (Domain 5, select TWO)** — An extraction system reports 97% aggregate accuracy,
and leadership wants to cut human review of high-confidence extractions. Which TWO
practices must precede that decision?
A) Accept the 97% aggregate figure as sufficient evidence on its own
B) Segment accuracy by document type and field, verifying consistent performance
   across all segments, since aggregate metrics can mask systematic failures on
   specific types
